        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is elevation the same as facade?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Not exactly, though they're closely related and often used interchangeably in everyday conversation. Elevation technically refers to a flat, measured architectural drawing of one side of a building, while facade refers to the actual built exterior surface."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why do architects use elevation drawings if the facade is what actually gets built?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Elevation drawings let architects and engineers communicate proportions, material zones, and dimensions precisely before construction begins, something a photograph or render alone can't do with the same accuracy."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do I need to know the difference between elevation and facade to brief a designer?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Not strictly, since most professionals understand both terms to mean roughly the same thing in casual use. Knowing the distinction simply helps you communicate more precisely when reviewing drawings versus discussing the finished material appearance."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What's a front elevation?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A front elevation is a flat, straight-on architectural drawing of a building's front face, showing its height, proportions, and material zones without any angle or perspective distortion."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can a facade look different from its elevation drawing?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes, to some degree. An elevation drawing shows proportions and layout accurately, but real light, shadow, and material texture only fully reveal themselves once the facade is actually built, which is why 3D renders and material samples are important alongside elevation drawings."
        }
      }
